n *after* the landlord played ♦A — meaning they couldn\'t or chose not to beat an ace. So if they couldn’t beat an ace, they likely cannot beat a king or ace now either. Therefore, if we pass now, the teammate will have to pass again, and the landlord keeps control — which is bad.\n\nSo we **must** take this trick if we can, because no one else likely can. Among our options, playing ♦K is sufficient to beat ♠Q and conserves our higher cards (A and 2) for future critical moments (like blocking a bomb or stopping a run near endgame).\n\nReason: We should play ♦K — it beats the landlord’s ♠Q, takes control from the landlord, and saves our precious A and 2 for later when they may be more valuable. Our teammate likely lacks high singles (since they passed on ♦A), so letting the landlord keep playing would risk them continuing unchallenged.\n\nAnswer: {"cards": ["♦K"]}'}
