y 4 — but no, in standard rules, **only higher cards beat previous plays**. So if I play 4, only 5 or above can beat it.\n\nSo playing ♥4 (single) gives teammate a chance to *follow with a 3* — but wait, do 3s exist?\n\nFrom card counting: we know remaining unknowns: K:1, 8:1, 6:1, 4:2 — but that’s from unplayed cards perspective. However, 3s were never mentioned — many may already be played.\n\nBut regardless, **3 is lowest**, so if teammate has a 3, they could play it only if allowed — but no, you must beat the current play. So if I play 4, teammate cannot play 3 — only 5 or higher.\n\nSo playing a low card like 4 forces others to play higher.\n\nBut here\'s the dilemma: if I play a very low card (like 4), then teammate might not be able to beat it (if they only have high cards), but they could pass — then landlord passes — and I get another turn. That’s good — I can continue emptying.\n\nBut if I play a slightly higher card, I might block myself.\n\nBest strategy when near endgame and teammate also close: **play small to survive and force opponent to use valuable cards**.\n\nAlso, note: I have ♣2 — the highest single (only red joker > black joker > 2). Playing that now would lose control immediately.\n\nSo best move: play the **lowest single possible**: a 4.\n\nI hold two 4s — playing one single 4 allows me to keep the other 4 for pairing later, perhaps.\n\nAlternatively, playing the pair of 4s might be better if I want to get rid of both early — but again, very low pair — unlikely to win unless others pass.\n\nBut if I play pair of 4s, and teammate has a low pair (e.g., 5s), they can’t play under — must play higher. So same issue.\n\nBut crucially: **if I play a single**, there\'s a chance the teammate can match or complement by playing a card that helps cycle back to me.\n\nBut ultimate goal: prevent landlord from going out.\n\nSince landlord has only 2 cards, they likely will go out on their next one or two turns unless interrupted.\n\nSo I must try to take control — meaning: play something small, hope both pass, so I can play again.\n\nTherefore, playing a **very low card** increases chance that others will pass (because they don’t want to waste a good card on a 4).\n\nSo playing **♥4** (single) is ideal: it\'s the lowest card I can play, maximizes chance that both opponents pass, giving me another turn to play again — possibly leading another round.\n\nIf I instead play a higher card like ♣2, I waste it and probably lose control.\n\nSame with ♥A — too high.\n\nSo best move: **single ♥4**\n\nReason: Playing the lowest possible card (♥4) maximizes the chance that both the landlord and my teammate pass, allowing me to retain initiative and play again on the next round. This is critical because the landlord is close to winning (only 2 cards left), and I need to disrupt their momentum. By playing small, I conserve higher cards for future rounds and increase pressure on the landlord to reveal their holdings.\n\nAnswer: {"cards": ["♥4"]}'}
